A||N||5||RiceBurner515||Dyno Engines||||1||
Z||000000||RiceBurner515||11-29-2000||10:59 PM||||2 quick questions:<P>1) What are those dyno's? Is it like a program that you put in all the info and it tells you HP and Torque at certain RPM's? Where can i get one? Is it a program for a computer? I want one....<P>2) What exactly do TDC (3.4), DOHC (3.5), S/C (3.8 & 4.3), Crate (3.4), Pushrod (3.4), Overbore (3.6), and Series 2 (3.8) mean? What are the differences? Sorry if its a long answer response.<P>Thanks A LOT!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||RiceBurner515||11-29-2000||11:01 PM||||I have a 2.5 4cyl 5spd and i want to do an engine swap for speed purposes, which one would be the cheapest to buy at a junkyard with average mileage? Most expensive? thanks again.....||||reg||1
Z||000002||Hank is Here||11-30-2000||12:06 AM||||RiceBurner, <BR>I'll tackle the engine names for you since that is fairly non technical.  Anyone please correct me if I am wrong  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>  <BR>DOHC-Dual overhead cam=TDC-Twin dual cam both the same engine family (I believe)<P>S/C-supercharged-the only commonly s/c enine used in a Fiero is the 3800 Buick engine- I believe it has it roots back to the early 60's in the 198 cid when it was a true Buick engine not a BOP (Buick Olds Pontiac) bolt pattern  also back then it was not s/c<P>Crate-a term used for any NEW motor ordered from the factory (GM)-these are not rebuilt units<P>Pushrod 3.4-a standard pushrod engine-no overhead cam, same family as the stock 2.8 in the Fiero,  also in the family the 3.1, a 3.6 can be made by boring out a 3.4 <P>Series (aka Gen or Generation) II 3800- there is no Series II 3.8  Denoting which generation Buick engine it is 3.8's generally from the 80's early 90's, 3800 series I mid 90's, series II current 3800 engine II.  I am not sure but I believe all series I and II are s/c, if they are not they are just called a 3800 correct?  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||william_thorniii||11-30-2000||12:13 AM||||Rice,<P>I believe the type of dynamometer you are asking about is the virtual dyno.  In other words it is a computer program that "theorhetically" builds an engine and gives you an idea of the output.  <P>The 3.4 TDC is the second generation in the current DOHC line up.  The grandfather was the Quad 4, then came the TDC (twin dual cam) then the Nortstar and lastly the Shortstar.  The TDC came in various GM cars (primarily W-bodies) in the early to mid 90's. <P>The 3.5L DOHC or commonly referred to as "Shortstar" is a new Olds/Caddy engine.  It made its debut in the 1998 Olds Intrigue sedan.  It is very similar to the TDC in that it was created from the Northstar and the Northstar was created from the TDC.  <P>The pushrod 3.4L is yet another 60 degree v-6.  It is virtually the same engine as the stock Fiero 2.8L 6.  The block casting is slightly larger to accomodate a larger displacement, but the top end from the 2.8 bolts up to a 3.4L block without modifications.  <P>The 3.6 is a 3.4L with the cylendars bored slightly .  <P>The 3800 series II is the third generation of the Buick 3.8L engine.  The series I and II are very different from the original 3.8L's though.  It is GM's current engine of choice and can be found in natually aspirated form in virtually every mid-size, sedan and mini-van that the General produces.  It is more similar to the 3.4 and 3.6L engines as it is a cam in block (hence the pushrod terminology).  <P>The differences are absolutely astronomical.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||5
Z||000004||1FST2M6||11-30-2000||10:04 AM||||for Dyno informations check out <A HREF="http://www.dynojet.com" TARGET=_blank>www.dynojet.com</A>  or <A HREF="http://www.engineered.net" TARGET=_blank>www.engineered.net</A>  (local speed shop)<P>a dyno is a 3600lb drum that you drive your car onto... strap it down and hook a lead to the ign coil. the RPMs of the motor are read by a computer and the RPMs of the drums the drive tires are spinning and the time it takes the car to Redline will determine the HP and Torque.. i can send you a movie of my car on the dyno if you'd like.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||11-30-2000||02:04 PM||||The 3.5 DOHC (Shortstar), all flavors of the 3.8/3800, and the 4.3 have a 90 degree bank angle, while the 3.4 TDC, 2.8, 3.1, 3.4, 3.6 pushrod engines have 60 degree bank angles.<P>||||reg||1
